  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**\nThis solution builds upon the parent program but introduces several improvements to handle sequence normalization more effectively. The core idea is to maintain the scale invariance property of the objective function (based on the given evaluation function formula) by normalizing sequences only in the evaluator, not in the optimization process. This eliminates issues arising from floating-point precision and prevents sequences from exceeding the coefficient cap unintentionally.\n\nKey improvements include:\n   - Independent control over length normalization and sequence normalization.\n   - Explicit sequence normalization after favorable sample evaluation.\n   - Consistent objective calculation through the evaluator's defense mechanisms.\n\nHowever, we must consider the evaluation cost scaling with longer sequences. The new objective metric is better with more favorable samples but the computation cost increases with sequence length. This requires a trade-off between sequence quality and computation feasibility.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**\nFor a sequence `a` of length `n`, the objective is defined as:\n\n```python\ndef evaluate_sequence(sequence: list[float]) -> float:\n    # Enhanced version\n    conv = full_linear_auto_convolve(sequence)\n    max_conv = np.max(conv)\n    sum_seq = np.sum(sequence)\n    if sum_seq < 0.01:  # Defensive fallback\n        sum_seq = 1.0\n        # Recalculate convolution?\n        sum_adjusted = sum_seq\n        max_conv = ...  # Compute again?\n        return float(2 * n * max_conv / (sum_adjusted ** 2))\n```\n\nWhere `n` is the length of the sequence.\n\nFor optimization:\n   - Primary sample: Maintain sequence at 1.0 sum.\n   - Secondary sample: Normalize sequence to handle equilibria.\n   - Evaluate both against the objective function.\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport time\n\ndef precompute_evaluations():\n    # First parent-like evaluation\n    seq_parent = np.ones(base_length) / base_length\n    parent_score = evaluate_sequence(seq_parent.tolist())\n    \n    # Also include new evaluation\n    # But note the max convolution is now at full scale\n\ndef optimized_direction_vectors():\n    # Uniform initialization\n    uniform = np.ones(parent_length) / parent_length\n    \n    # Edge-concentrated initialization\n    edge = np.exp(-0.5 * ((np.arange(parent_length) - (parent_length-1)/2) / (0.5*parent_length))**2)\n    edge = edge / np.sum(edge)\n    edge = project_simplex(edge)  # Ensures nonnegativity\n    \n    # Now combine uniformly and edge and any others\n\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42, budget_s=1800, **kwargs):\n    base_length_default = 384\n    if 'height_sequence_1' not in kwargs:\n        parent_seq = None\n\n    # Extract parent\n    if parent_seq is not None and np.sum(parent_seq) > 1e-10:\n        parent_len = len(parent_seq)\n        base_length = parent_len\n    else:\n        base_length = base_length_default\n\n    # Generate candidates\n    best_seq = None\n    best_score = float('inf')\n\n    # Candidate initialization 1 (uniform)\n    cand1 = np.ones(base_length) / base_length\n    cand1_score = evaluate_sequence(cand1.tolist())\n\n    candidates = [cand1]\n    # Candidate initialization 2 (edge)\n    try:\n        edge_w = np.minimum(np.arange(base_length), base_length - 1 - np.arange(base_length))\n        edge_w = edge_w.astype(np.float64) / edge_w.mean()\n        edge_w = project_simplex(edge_w)\n        candidates.append(edge_w)\n    except:\n        pass\n\n    # Candidate initialization 3 (length-normalized)\n    if base_length != base_length_default or ... :\n        # Safety adjustment based on provided parent\n\n    # Computing resource management\n    remaining_time = budget_s\n    time_per_optimization = 500  # Per Frank-Wolfe optimization iteration\n\n    for cand in candidates:\n        # Ensure cand is valid (only after processing)\n        cand = project_non_negativity(cand)\n        \n        # Apply the optimization\n        cand_opt, new_score, iteration_time = enhanced_fw_optimization(cand, time_per_optimization)\n        \n        # Then evaluate the polished candidate\n        cand_rop = sg_polish(cand_opt, time_remaining, precision=...)\n\n    # Include fallback: if no parent is available, use base_length_default\n    if (best_score is None or best_score > current_best) and ... :\n        fallback_seq = np.ones(base_length_default) / base_length_default\n        fallback_score = evaluate_sequence(fallback_seq.tolist())\n        best_seq, best_score = fallback_seq, fallback_score\n\n    # Final adjustment for coefficient cap\n    if np.max(best_seq) > 1000:\n        best_seq = best_seq / (best_seq / 1000).max()\n\n    return best_seq.tolist(), best_score\n\ndef enhanced_fw_optimization(seq, time_limit):\n    # This function implements the Frank-Wolfe algorithm with adaptive scaling\n    # Initial setup\n    n = len(seq)\n    \n    # Project to simplex (non-neg) but avoid altering sequence\n    normalized_seq = project_simplex(seq)\n    \n    # Measure time per iteration\n    conv_val = evaluate_sequence(normalized_seq.tolist())\n    \n    # Main Frank-Wolfe iteration with time constraint\n    alpha = 0.001  # Initial stepsize\n    obj_best = 2 * n * conv_val_max / (sum_val ** 2)\n    \n    # Additional iteration scheduling\n    update_frequency = 200\n```\nDetails to be refined: The edge-concentrated initialization needs further definition. The full_linear_auto_convolve function is essential as it is not identical to the parent program.",
